A||||8||Homicidal||Do I need to bleed the clutch??????||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||04-25-2000||01:55 AM||||I got the piece I needed for my clutch lever.   But the car still won't start.   The clutch pedal feels lower than before.  When i press it in, it doesn't have the pressure it used to.<P>I am assuming this is because I opened the clutch fluid reservoir and cleaned it out which pushed air into it or maybe air got into the clutch other ways.  Either way, is this the symptom of a clutch that needs bleeding?  Would it prevent the clutch from moving in all the way and prevent it from starting in some cases?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||04-25-2000||02:03 AM||||Yes you need to bleed the clutch cylinders. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Homicidal||04-25-2000||02:08 AM||||So not bleeding the cylinders would prevent the car from starting?||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||04-25-2000||08:45 AM||||As long as it is in neutral, and you are pushing the clutch pedal down, it should start right up, reguardless of air in the system.<P>You might want to check the clutch start switch thing. Its a rod that is pushed by the clutch pedal just under the dash.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Homicidal||04-25-2000||12:04 PM||||I did it!  Thanks SO much for the switch help!<P>But my clutch pedal STILL feels soft, so I must bleed the system I'm sure.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Princess46||04-25-2000||10:59 PM||||If it is still soft, then bleed it AGAIN!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fret Naught||04-26-2000||01:18 AM||||Say you didn't by chance flush your hydraulic lines out with soap did you? I just got an e-mail from a gal named Erika, asking about clutch problems said her friend washed the lines out with soap and water.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Homicidal||04-26-2000||02:53 AM||||Yup, my girl.  But ugh, she TOTALLY mis-understood!<P>I had a BIG buildup of gunk in the master cylinder resevoir and I sprayed a SMALL touch of carbeurator (sp?) choke cleaner to get it off.  Then I made sure I wiped all around the inside and got it all out and then poured new fluid into it.  <P>But NO soap+water! ||||reg||1
Z||000008||fieroshop||04-26-2000||12:56 PM||||If any of you want to clean out the lines and or master and slave the best thing to use is alcohol. I use alcohol and run it thru the system, then I blow out the lines with an air nozzle, ( make sure that the bleeder valve is open.) to dry it out and then use hydraulic oil and bleed the system completely. ||||reg||1
